{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>I am YHWH your Elohim.</b> What kind of utterance is this, and what commandment and what warning is this? And if you ask — 'I am YHWH your Elohim,' and I command you to know with clear knowledge that He is the Creator, and that I am He who commands all the commandments, and that He is faithful to exact retribution for good and for evil, and the knowledge of the Name that a person is obligated to know — that He is the Creator, blessed be He. And Rabbi Tanhuma expounds: Since I revealed myself to you at the sea as a mighty one and a man of war, and on the day of the giving of the Torah as an elder full of mercy — do not think that there are two authorities, but 'I' and there is no other. Another interpretation: Since when the Holy One, blessed be He, revealed himself on the day of the giving of the Torah, he revealed himself with multitudes — 'רבותי אלפי שנאן' — and Israel would set one against the other, the Creator said to them: ״אנכי ה׳״ — I am one. And he also opened for them seven heavens and showed them that He is one, as it is said: ״אתה הראת לדעת כי ה׳ הוא האלהים אין עוד מלבדו״. And therefore ״אנכי״ and ״לא יהיה לך״ were said in singular form, in order to teach a defense for Israel in the matter of the calf, as Rashi explained, that Moses said in that matter: ״ואל תענשהו כי לי לבדי הזהרת לא יהיה לך״ — and You did not say לא יהיה לכם. And further it is said in the Midrash that therefore all of them were said in singular form, because the Holy One, blessed be He, foresaw that individuals would come to violate them: Micah violates ״אנכי״; Jeroboam violates ״לא יהיה לך״; Zelophehad — ״זכור״; Joab — ״לא תרצח״; Amnon — ״לא תנאף״; Achan — ״לא תגנוב״; Ziba — ״לא תענה״; Ahab — ״לא תחמוד״. Therefore all of them were said in singular form. Another interpretation: ״אנכי ה׳ אלהיך״ — at the time of the giving of the Torah the voice went forth from the east, and Israel heard and came to receive the Torah; they came to the east — it went to the west; they came to the west — it went to the north; they came to the north — it went to the south; they came to the south — it came to them from heaven, as it is said: ״מן השמים השמיעך את קולו״. And Israel would say: ״והחכמה מאין תמצא״; the deep said: ״לא בי״. Israel began hearing from the four winds of the world, and from heaven and earth, and they said: perhaps, God forbid, there are many authorities — therefore it is written ״אנכי ה׳״ — all that you hear comes only from me. And from alef of ״אנכי״ to kaf of ״לרעך״ there are 613 letters, corresponding to the 613 commandments. And in chapter 1 of Kiddushin we say: At the time the Holy One, blessed be He, said ״אנכי״ and ״לא יהיה לך״, the nations of the world said: For Himself He seeks. Once they heard ״כבד את אביך ואת אמך״, they conceded regarding the first ones, as it is said: ״יודוך ה׳ כל מלכי ארץ כי שמעו אמרי פיך״. And further it is written ״ראש דברך אמת״ — and not ״סוף דברך״, but from ״סוף דברך״ they conceded that ״ראש דברך״ is truth."], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :register {:flags {0 ["God"]}}, :notes {0 "זכור paired with צלפחד is puzzling; the Midrashic tradition here apparently links each individual to a specific Decalogue commandment, but the rationale for Zelophehad and זכור is textually obscure. The phrase רבותי אלפי שנאן appears to cite Deuteronomy 33:2. The final word 'אמת' does not appear in the source text of Psalm 119:160 and may reflect a variant reading or paraphrase."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:03:44.840447767Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}